(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-12"] = {"chem-12-12-m01":{"idea":"Aldehydes and ketones both have C=O. They differ in what is on the carbonyl carbon.","steps":["In an aldehyde, the carbonyl carbon carries at least one hydrogen.","The group is written –CHO.","The carbonyl carbon is joined to one hydrogen and one R group (or two hydrogens in methanal)."],"others":"The group with the carbonyl carbon joined to two carbons is the ketone group. The –OH group is the alcohol group. The –COOH group, with an OH on the carbonyl carbon, is the carboxylic acid group.","trap":"Aldehyde has H on the C=O carbon. Ketone does not.","answer":"–CHO, with the carbonyl carbon joined to one hydrogen"},"chem-12-12-m02":{"idea":"A ketone has its C=O between two carbon groups.","steps":["The carbonyl carbon is joined to R and R′.","So the formula is R–CO–R′.","R and R′ may be the same or different."],"others":"R–CHO has a hydrogen on the carbonyl carbon, so it is an aldehyde. R–COOH is a carboxylic acid. R–O–R′ has no C=O at all, so it is an ether.","answer":"R–CO–R′"},"chem-12-12-m03":{"idea":"Aldehyde names end in -al, and the aldehyde carbon is always carbon 1.","steps":["CH₃CH₂CHO has three carbons, so the parent is propane.","Drop the -e and add -al: propanal.","The CHO carbon is always C-1, so no number is needed."],"others":"Propanoic acid has a COOH group, not CHO. Propanone is the ketone, CH₃COCH₃. Propan-1-al writes an unneeded locant, which is not the correct form.","trap":"No locant for -al: the carbon is always 1.","answer":"propanal"},"chem-12-12-m04":{"idea":"Ketone names end in -one.","steps":["CH₃COCH₃ has three carbons, so the parent is propane.","Replace -e with -one: propanone.","The C=O can only be on the middle carbon, so no number is needed."],"others":"Dimethyl ether has no C=O. Propan-1-one cannot exist as a ketone, as carbon 1 is at the end of the chain. Propanal is the aldehyde with the same three carbons.","answer":"propanone"},"chem-12-12-m05":{"idea":"Name the longest chain that includes the –COOH carbon, then place the branch.","steps":["The carboxyl carbon is C-1.","The chain is C-1 (COOH), C-2 (CH), C-3 (CH₃): three carbons, so propanoic acid.","The methyl group is on C-2.","The name is 2-methylpropanoic acid."],"others":"3-methylpropanoic acid puts the methyl on the end carbon, which would make a longer chain. Butanoic acid is the straight-chain isomer. 2-methylpropanal is an aldehyde, not an acid.","trap":"Choose the longest chain, then the lowest number.","answer":"2-methylpropanoic acid"},"chem-12-12-m06":{"idea":"The Rosenmund reduction makes an aldehyde from an acid chloride.","steps":["RCOCl + H₂ over Pd/BaSO₄ gives RCHO + HCl.","The Cl is replaced by H.","The reduction stops at the aldehyde."],"others":"An aldehyde to an alcohol needs NaBH₄ or LiAlH₄. A nitrile to a ketone would need a Grignard reagent. A carboxylic acid to a primary alcohol needs LiAlH₄.","answer":"an acyl chloride into an aldehyde"},"chem-12-12-m07":{"idea":"A poisoned catalyst is weaker, so it stops the reaction early.","steps":["Plain palladium would reduce RCOCl to RCHO and then on to RCH₂OH.","BaSO₄ lowers the activity of the palladium.","So the second reduction does not happen.","The aldehyde is the product."],"others":"Removing HCl is not the job of the poison. A poison slows the catalyst, not speeds it up. Oxidising the aldehyde to acid would be the opposite of a reduction.","trap":"Poison = weaker catalyst = stops at aldehyde.","answer":"stop the reduction at the aldehyde and prevent the formation of the alcohol"},"chem-12-12-m08":{"idea":"The Etard reaction is a controlled oxidation of a methyl group.","steps":["Toluene reacts with chromyl chloride, CrO₂Cl₂, in CS₂.","A chromium complex forms.","Hydrolysis of the complex gives benzaldehyde.","The oxidation stops at CHO."],"others":"Acetyl chloride with AlCl₃ is Friedel–Crafts acylation. Acidified KMnO₄ would oxidise toluene all the way to benzoic acid. CO and HCl with AlCl₃ is the Gatterman–Koch reaction.","trap":"Etard: CrO₂Cl₂. Gatterman–Koch: CO + HCl.","answer":"chromyl chloride, CrO₂Cl₂, followed by hydrolysis"},"chem-12-12-m09":{"idea":"CO and HCl together act as a source of the –CHO group.","steps":["Benzene with CO and HCl, AlCl₃ and CuCl is the Gatterman–Koch reaction.","The group –CHO replaces one H on the ring.","The product is benzaldehyde."],"others":"Acetophenone needs acetyl chloride (Friedel–Crafts). Toluene would come from a methyl group, not CO. Benzoic acid needs further oxidation.","answer":"benzaldehyde"},"chem-12-12-m10":{"idea":"Acetyl chloride and AlCl₃ attach a CH₃CO– group to the ring.","steps":["This is Friedel–Crafts acylation.","CH₃CO⁺ is the electrophile.","It replaces one H of benzene.","The product is C₆H₅COCH₃, acetophenone."],"others":"Benzoic acid has a –COOH group. Toluene would need CH₃Cl for alkylation. Benzaldehyde has only –CHO, with no methyl on the carbonyl.","answer":"acetophenone"},"chem-12-12-m11":{"idea":"Ozonolysis cuts the C=C bond and puts C=O on each carbon.","steps":["Propene is CH₃–CH=CH₂.","The CH₃CH= end becomes CH₃CHO, ethanal.","The =CH₂ end becomes HCHO, methanal.","Zn stops any further oxidation to acids."],"others":"Propanone would come from a carbon with two alkyl groups, which propene lacks. Propanal and methanol is not what C=C cleavage gives. Ethanoic acid would form only without zinc.","trap":"Each end of C=C becomes a C=O.","answer":"ethanal and methanal"},"chem-12-12-m12":{"idea":"Alkyne hydration follows Markovnikov's rule and gives an enol first.","steps":["Water adds to propyne, CH₃–C≡CH, with H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄.","–OH goes to the middle carbon, giving CH₃C(OH)=CH₂.","An enol is unstable and shifts at once to the keto form.","The product is propanone, CH₃COCH₃."],"others":"Propanoic acid needs oxidation, which does not happen here. Propan-1-ol is an alcohol. Propanal would need –OH on the end carbon, which is only the case for ethyne giving ethanal.","trap":"Only ethyne gives an aldehyde.","answer":"propanone"},"chem-12-12-m13":{"idea":"At similar mass, stronger hydrogen bonding gives a higher boiling point.","steps":["Propanoic acid forms dimers, with two hydrogen bonds per pair.","Butan-1-ol forms hydrogen bonds with one neighbour.","Butanone has only dipole–dipole forces, and diethyl ether even less.","So the order is acid > alcohol > ketone > ether."],"others":"Diethyl ether is polar but has no hydrogen bonding, so it boils lowest. Butanone has only dipole forces. Butan-1-ol is high, but it comes second to the acid.","answer":"propanoic acid, which forms hydrogen-bonded dimers"},"chem-12-12-m14":{"idea":"Acid molecules pair up through two hydrogen bonds.","steps":["Two –COOH groups bond to each other at both O–H and C=O.","This makes a dimer, even in the vapour.","The dimer acts like a molecule of twice the mass.","So more energy is needed to boil it."],"others":"The C=O group does not form covalent links between molecules. Acids are covalent, not ionic, in the liquid. Their dipole moment is not the main cause of the higher boiling point.","answer":"form hydrogen-bonded dimers, even in the vapour phase"},"chem-12-12-m15":{"idea":"Ketones are less reactive because the carbonyl carbon is less positive and more crowded.","steps":["Alkyl groups push electrons towards the carbonyl carbon.","Two alkyl groups reduce its partial positive charge.","Two bulky groups also block the nucleophile.","An aldehyde has one alkyl group at most, so it is more reactive."],"others":"Alkyl groups donate electrons; they do not withdraw them. The carbonyl group in ketones is polar. A hydrogen on the carbonyl carbon helps the attack, not blocks it, and ketones lack it.","answer":"two electron-donating alkyl groups, and more steric crowding on the carbonyl carbon"},"chem-12-12-m16":{"idea":"The fewer the alkyl groups on C=O, the faster the nucleophilic addition.","steps":["Methanal has two H and no alkyl group.","It has the highest positive charge and the least crowding.","Ethanal has one methyl group, and benzaldehyde has a ring that donates by resonance.","Propanone has two alkyl groups, so it is the slowest."],"others":"Ethanal is less reactive because of its methyl group. Benzaldehyde is less reactive still because of ring resonance. Propanone has two groups and is the least reactive of these.","trap":"Order: HCHO > aliphatic aldehyde > aromatic aldehyde > ketone.","answer":"HCHO, methanal"},"chem-12-12-m17":{"idea":"Tollens' reagent oxidises aldehydes and is reduced to silver.","steps":["Ethanal is an aldehyde, so it is oxidised to ethanoate.","Ag⁺ in the reagent is reduced to Ag metal.","The silver coats the glass as a mirror.","Ketones are not oxidised."],"others":"Benzophenone, acetophenone and propanone are all ketones, so none of them gives the mirror.","trap":"Silver mirror means aldehyde.","answer":"ethanal"},"chem-12-12-m18":{"idea":"Fehling's reagent is milder than Tollens' reagent.","steps":["Aromatic aldehydes are not oxidised by Fehling's solution.","They do reduce Tollens' reagent.","They also give Schiff's test and form oximes like all carbonyl compounds."],"others":"Oxime formation works with any carbonyl compound. Schiff's test is positive for aldehydes. Tollens' test is positive for benzaldehyde, so those three are all given.","trap":"Fehling's test is given by aliphatic aldehydes only.","answer":"Fehling's test"},"chem-12-12-m19":{"idea":"The iodoform test needs a CH₃–CO– group or a CH₃–CH(OH)– group.","steps":["Propanone is CH₃–CO–CH₃, so it has CH₃CO–.","Propanal and pentan-3-one have CH₃CH₂–CO, which is not CH₃–CO.","Methanal has no methyl group at all.","So only propanone is positive."],"others":"Methanal has no CH₃. Propanal has CH₃CH₂–CHO, and the carbon next to CO has no methyl group on it. Pentan-3-one has two ethyl groups, not a methyl on C=O.","trap":"Look for CH₃ directly on the carbonyl carbon.","answer":"propanone, CH₃COCH₃"},"chem-12-12-m20":{"idea":"Aldol condensation needs a hydrogen on the carbon next to C=O (α-hydrogen).","steps":["Base removes an α-hydrogen to make an enolate ion.","The enolate attacks another carbonyl compound.","Ethanal has three α-hydrogens, so it reacts.","The other three have no α-hydrogen."],"others":"2,2-dimethylpropanal has no H on the carbon next to CHO. Methanal has no carbon next to its C=O. Benzaldehyde has the ring carbon next to CHO, which carries no hydrogen.","answer":"ethanal, which has α-hydrogen atoms"},"chem-12-12-m21":{"idea":"Cannizzaro needs an aldehyde with no α-hydrogen.","steps":["Benzaldehyde has no α-hydrogen.","In concentrated alkali, one molecule is oxidised to the acid salt.","Another is reduced to the alcohol.","Aldehydes with α-hydrogen do aldol reactions instead."],"others":"Propanone is a ketone and has α-hydrogens. Ethanal and propanal have α-hydrogens, so they give aldol instead.","trap":"No α-H: Cannizzaro. With α-H: aldol.","answer":"benzaldehyde, which has no α-hydrogen"},"chem-12-12-m22":{"idea":"Two reductions turn C=O into CH₂, one in acid and one in base.","steps":["Clemmensen uses zinc amalgam and concentrated HCl.","It works in acid.","Wolff–Kishner does the same job in base.","So zinc amalgam with HCl is the answer."],"others":"Hydrazine and KOH in glycol is Wolff–Kishner. H₂ over Pd/BaSO₄ is Rosenmund. LiAlH₄ in dry ether gives alcohols, not CH₂.","trap":"Clemmensen: acid. Wolff–Kishner: base.","answer":"zinc amalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid"},"chem-12-12-m23":{"idea":"NaBH₄ reduces C=O to CH–OH.","steps":["C=O gains two H, one on carbon and one on oxygen.","A ketone R–CO–R′ gives R–CH(OH)–R′.","The carbon has two carbon neighbours.","This is a secondary alcohol."],"others":"An alkane needs Clemmensen or Wolff–Kishner. A primary alcohol comes from an aldehyde. A carboxylic acid is an oxidation product, not a reduction product.","answer":"a secondary alcohol"},"chem-12-12-m24":{"idea":"Acid strength depends on the stability of the conjugate base.","steps":["The carboxylate ion has two equal C–O bonds.","The charge is shared equally by two oxygens.","In the phenoxide ion, the charge goes onto ring carbons, with less stabilisation.","So the acid is stronger than the phenol."],"others":"Hydrogen bonding in acids is not absent. Phenoxide structures are not equivalent. The O–H bond in phenol is not weaker than in an acid.","answer":"the carboxylate ion is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures"},"chem-12-12-m25":{"idea":"Chlorine pulls electrons and stabilises the carboxylate ion.","steps":["Cl has a –I effect.","More Cl atoms mean a stronger pull.","Three Cl atoms stabilise the anion most.","So Cl₃CCOOH, pKa about 0.65, is the strongest."],"others":"One Cl and two Cl atoms are less stabilising than three. CH₃COOH has a methyl group that pushes electrons, so it is the weakest at pKa 4.76.","trap":"More Cl, lower pKa, stronger acid.","answer":"Cl₃CCOOH"},"chem-12-12-m26":{"idea":"A higher pKa means a weaker acid.","steps":["The pKa values are 3.75, 4.76, 4.20 and 2.86.","The highest, 4.76, belongs to acetic acid.","The methyl group pushes electrons and destabilises the anion.","So acetic acid is the weakest of the four."],"others":"Chloroacetic acid has the lowest pKa and is the strongest. Formic acid has pKa 3.75, so it is stronger than acetic acid. Benzoic acid has 4.20, also stronger.","trap":"Higher pKa, weaker acid.","answer":"CH₃COOH, with pKa 4.76"},"chem-12-12-m27":{"idea":"Carboxylic acids release CO₂ from sodium bicarbonate.","steps":["Acids are strong enough to react with NaHCO₃.","RCOOH + NaHCO₃ → RCOONa + H₂O + CO₂, with fizzing.","Phenols are too weak and give no CO₂.","So the fizzing test separates the two."],"others":"Neutral FeCl₃ gives violet with phenols, not with acids. Sodium nitroprusside gives a red colour with some ketones. Tollens' reagent is for aldehydes.","trap":"Fizzing with NaHCO₃ means acid.","answer":"brisk effervescence of CO₂ with aqueous sodium bicarbonate"},"chem-12-12-n01":{"idea":"The molar mass is the sum of atomic masses.","steps":["Propanal is CH₃CH₂CHO, which is C₃H₆O.","Mass = 3 × 12 + 6 × 1 + 16.","= 36 + 6 + 16 = 58 g/mol.","Check: CₙH₂ₙO has 14n + 16 = 58 for n = 3."],"answer":"58 g/mol"},"chem-12-12-n02":{"idea":"Add the atomic masses of C₇H₆O₂.","steps":["Benzoic acid is C₆H₅COOH, which is C₇H₆O₂.","Mass = 7 × 12 + 6 × 1 + 2 × 16.","= 84 + 6 + 32 = 122 g/mol.","Check: C₆H₅ is 77 and COOH is 45, which is 122."],"answer":"122 g/mol"},"chem-12-12-n03":{"idea":"Acid and bicarbonate give one mole of CO₂ per mole of acid.","steps":["CH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂.","Moles of acid = 6 / 60 = 0.1 mol.","Moles of CO₂ = 0.1 mol.","Volume = 0.1 × 22.4 = 2.24 L."],"answer":"2.24 L"},"chem-12-12-n04":{"idea":"Acid and NaOH react 1 to 1.","steps":["Moles of acid = 3 / 60 = 0.05 mol.","Moles of NaOH needed = 0.05 mol.","Volume = moles / M = 0.05 / 0.5 = 0.1 L.","0.1 L = 100 mL."],"trap":"Convert litres to mL at the end.","answer":"100 mL"},"chem-12-12-n05":{"idea":"For a monobasic acid, moles of acid equal moles of NaOH.","steps":["Moles of NaOH = 0.020 L × 0.5 M = 0.010 mol.","Moles of acid = 0.010 mol.","Molar mass = 1.22 g / 0.010 mol = 122 g/mol.","This fits benzoic acid."],"answer":"122 g/mol"},"chem-12-12-n06":{"idea":"Mass percent is the element's mass divided by the molar mass.","steps":["Propanone is C₃H₆O, molar mass = 36 + 6 + 16 = 58.","Oxygen = 16.","% O = 16 / 58 × 100 = 27.59.","Check: C and H are 42 / 58 = 72.41 percent, and 100 − 72.41 = 27.59."],"answer":"27.59 %"},"chem-12-12-n07":{"idea":"Aldol is an addition product, so its mass equals the reactant mass at 100 percent yield.","steps":["Moles of ethanal = 4.4 / 44 = 0.1 mol.","2 mol of ethanal give 1 mol of aldol, so 0.05 mol (88 g/mol) = 4.4 g.","At 80 percent yield: 4.4 × 0.80 = 3.52 g.","Check: the aldol mass equals the ethanal mass used."],"answer":"3.52 g"},"chem-12-12-n08":{"idea":"Each aldehyde molecule gives 2 silver atoms.","steps":["Moles of ethanal = 2.2 / 44 = 0.05 mol.","Moles of Ag = 2 × 0.05 = 0.10 mol.","Mass of Ag = 0.10 × 108 = 10.8 g.","Check: the aldehyde gives up 2 electrons and each Ag⁺ takes 1."],"answer":"10.8 g"},"chem-12-12-n09":{"idea":"One mole of propanone gives one mole of iodoform.","steps":["Moles of propanone = 5.8 / 58 = 0.1 mol.","Moles of CHI₃ = 0.1 mol.","Molar mass of CHI₃ = 12 + 1 + 3 × 127 = 394 g/mol.","Mass = 0.1 × 394 = 39.4 g."],"answer":"39.4 g"},"chem-12-12-n10":{"idea":"Count the isomers that fit C₄H₈O with a C=O group in an open chain.","steps":["Aldehydes: butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO.","Aldehydes: 2-methylpropanal, (CH₃)₂CHCHO.","Ketones: butanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃.","Total = 2 + 1 = 3."],"trap":"Do not count rings or ethers.","answer":"3"},"chem-12-12-n11":{"idea":"Degree of unsaturation counts rings plus double bonds.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 − H) / 2.","For C₇H₆O: (14 + 2 − 6) / 2 = 5.","Check: 1 ring + 3 C=C in the ring + 1 C=O = 5."],"answer":"5"}};
